(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-physiology-master-exam-version-7"]={"title":"Physiology Master Exam — Form C (70 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Blood serves three broad roles — transport, regulation, and protection. Which pairing correctly matches a component to one of these roles?","choices":["Platelets transport dietary nutrients, while the red cells are what defend against infection","White cells regulate body temperature through inflammatory signaling, while plasma proteins carry oxygen","Plasma dissolves most oxygen for transport, while the red cells are what actually clot the blood","Red cells carry out transport of oxygen, while platelets and clotting proteins provide protection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Red cells (via hemoglobin) exemplify the transport role by carrying oxygen, while platelets and plasma clotting proteins serve the protective role by sealing damaged vessels; each component maps to a distinct one of blood's broad functions. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 3-6"},{"q":"Hemoglobin concentration is normally reported around 15 g/dL. This value reflects...","choices":["the number of white cells circulating per microliter","the fraction of hemoglobin currently bound to oxygen","the amount of oxygen-binding protein per volume of blood","the percentage of blood volume made up of red cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hemoglobin concentration measures how much oxygen-carrying protein is present per unit volume of blood; since each gram binds about 1.34 mL of O2, this value largely determines the blood's oxygen-carrying capacity. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 21"},{"q":"The sigmoid (S-shaped) oxygen-hemoglobin curve reflects that...","choices":["binding of one oxygen molecule increases hemoglobin's affinity for the next","oxygen binding is entirely independent and non-cooperative at each of the four sites","each hemoglobin molecule can bind only a single oxygen at any given time","hemoglobin binds oxygen and carbon dioxide at the exact same binding site"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hemoglobin binds oxygen cooperatively: attachment of one O2 changes the molecule's conformation and raises its affinity for subsequent O2, producing the characteristic S-shaped curve that favors loading in the lungs and unloading at tissues. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 21"},{"q":"All blood cell lines ultimately arise from...","choices":["endothelial cells lining the marrow sinusoidal vasculature","pluripotent hematopoietic stem cells in the bone marrow","mature lymphocytes that dedifferentiate in the spleen","circulating monocytes that divide within the blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Every blood cell lineage — red cells, all white cells, and platelets — derives from pluripotent hematopoietic stem cells in the marrow, which give rise to committed progenitors under the influence of specific growth factors. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 14-16"},{"q":"Adequate red cell production depends on vitamin B12, whose absorption in turn requires...","choices":["intrinsic factor secreted by the gastric parietal cells","pancreatic amylase acting in the duodenum","bile salts released from the gallbladder under these conditions","erythropoietin that is produced mainly by the kidneys"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vitamin B12 must bind intrinsic factor, made by gastric parietal cells, to be absorbed in the terminal ileum; loss of intrinsic factor (as in atrophic gastritis) causes B12 deficiency and the impaired red-cell maturation of pernicious anemia. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 17; GI B12 slides"},{"q":"Calcium ions are required at multiple steps of coagulation because they...","choices":["are the enzyme that converts prothrombin to thrombin","dissolve the clot completely once the healing process is fully complete","are needed for clotting factors to assemble on phospholipid surfaces","directly cleave fibrinogen into fibrin strands"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Calcium (factor IV) bridges vitamin K-dependent clotting factors to the negatively charged phospholipid surfaces of platelets, allowing the reaction complexes to assemble; without calcium, the cascade cannot proceed, which is why calcium chelation prevents clotting in a blood tube. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 40-47"},{"q":"The intrinsic pathway of coagulation is initiated when blood contacts...","choices":["erythropoietin that is secreted by the injured kidney into the blood","a negatively charged surface such as exposed subendothelial collagen","bile salts entering the bloodstream from the gut","tissue factor released from damaged extravascular cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The intrinsic (contact) pathway is triggered when blood is exposed to negatively charged surfaces like subendothelial collagen, which activates factor XII and begins the cascade using components already present in the blood. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 44-47"},{"q":"Once fibrin strands form, factor XIII strengthens the clot by...","choices":["activating platelets to release additional thromboxane A2 locally","dissolving excess fibrin to limit the clot's overall size","cross-linking adjacent fibrin strands into a stable meshwork","converting still more fibrinogen into new fibrin strands"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Activated fibrin-stabilizing factor (factor XIII) forms covalent cross-links between fibrin strands, converting the initially loose fibrin into a strong, stable three-dimensional meshwork that resists breakdown. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 40-47"},{"q":"Antithrombin III limits clotting by...","choices":["stimulating the platelets to aggregate together much faster","inactivating thrombin and other activated clotting factors","cross-linking fibrin to strengthen the clot","supplying vitamin K for new factor synthesis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Antithrombin III is a natural anticoagulant that binds and neutralizes thrombin and several other activated serine-protease factors, restraining the cascade and helping confine clot formation to the site of injury (its action is greatly accelerated by heparin). — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 48"},{"q":"Place the phases of hemostasis in their correct order.","choices":["Platelet plug, then fibrinolysis, then vascular spasm during this particular process","Coagulation first, then vascular spasm, and finally the platelet plug","Clot retraction, then platelet plug, then vascular spasm","Vascular spasm, then platelet plug, then coagulation and clot formation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hemostasis proceeds as immediate vascular spasm, followed by formation of the platelet plug (primary hemostasis), and then the coagulation cascade producing a fibrin clot (secondary hemostasis), which is later remodeled and dissolved. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 34-48"},{"q":"Circulating monocytes contribute to defense by...","choices":["forming the fibrin meshwork that walls off infection","producing antibodies against specific bacterial antigens","entering tissues and maturing into long-lived phagocytic macrophages","carrying dissolved oxygen to the sites of active infection in the tissues"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Monocytes circulate briefly, then migrate into tissues where they enlarge and mature into macrophages — powerful, long-lived phagocytes that can ingest many organisms, clear debris, and present antigen to lymphocytes. — Blood & Immunology, slides 13, 17, 30"},{"q":"Basophils and mast cells release histamine and heparin, which contribute to...","choices":["the inflammatory and allergic response","the direct killing of virus-infected cells","the synthesis of new antibodies in lymph nodes","the transport of oxygen within the blood"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Basophils and their tissue counterparts, mast cells, store histamine, heparin, and other mediators; when triggered (for example, by IgE cross-linking), they degranulate, driving the vasodilation, increased permeability, and other features of inflammation and immediate allergy. — Blood & Immunology, slides 34, 64-65"},{"q":"The spleen supports immunity mainly by...","choices":["filtering blood and removing pathogens and worn-out cells","manufacturing intrinsic factor needed for B12 uptake","secreting digestive enzymes into the bloodstream","producing digestive bile that destroys the ingested bacteria"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The spleen filters the blood: macrophages in its red pulp and sinuses remove circulating microbes, debris, and senescent red cells and platelets, while its white pulp houses lymphocytes that mount adaptive responses to blood-borne antigens. — Blood & Immunology, slide 24"},{"q":"Lymphocytes are unusual among leukocytes because they...","choices":["are unable to leave the bloodstream at any time","remain fixed in one tissue for their entire lifespan","survive only a few hours in the blood before being routinely replaced","recirculate continuously between blood, lymph, and lymphoid tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Unlike short-lived neutrophils, lymphocytes are long-lived and recirculate endlessly — from blood into lymphoid tissue, into lymph, and back to blood — which maximizes their chance of encountering the specific antigen they recognize. — Blood & Immunology, slide 12"},{"q":"The redness and warmth of acute inflammation result largely from...","choices":["loss of all blood flow entirely to the damaged tissue region","vasoconstriction that traps blood in the tissue","vasodilation and increased blood flow to the injured area","antibody deposition occurring within the vessel walls"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inflammatory mediators such as histamine cause local vasodilation and increased blood flow, which produce the redness and warmth; increased capillary permeability then adds the swelling characteristic of inflammation. — Blood & Immunology, slide 25"},{"q":"Cytotoxic (CD8) T-cells destroy virus-infected cells by...","choices":["coating the infected cell with antibody so it can be removed much later","engulfing the entire infected cell by phagocytosis","releasing perforins and enzymes that trigger the target cell's death","secreting histamine that lyses the infected cell"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cytotoxic T-cells recognize antigen presented on MHC I of an infected cell, then release perforins that form pores and enzymes that enter and induce the target cell's death, eliminating the reservoir of viral replication. — Blood & Immunology, slide 59"},{"q":"One direct action of the complement cascade is to...","choices":["form membrane pores that lyse the targeted microbe","generate memory cells for a faster second response","present antigen fragments to helper T-cells","synthesize antibodies specific to the pathogen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beyond opsonizing microbes and promoting inflammation, the complement cascade assembles a membrane attack complex that punches pores in the target's membrane, causing lysis — a direct killing mechanism of this innate system. — Blood & Immunology, slide 53"},{"q":"Compared with the primary response, the secondary antibody response is...","choices":["slower and weaker because memory cells have died off","limited to IgM with no IgG produced at all","faster, larger, and dominated by higher-affinity IgG","identical in speed and magnitude to the first"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Because memory B- and T-cells persist from the first exposure, a repeat encounter produces a response that is quicker, of greater magnitude, and dominated by high-affinity IgG rather than the IgM that led the primary response; this is the basis of booster immunizations. — Blood & Immunology, slide 48"},{"q":"Vaccination with a killed or attenuated organism works by...","choices":["permanently deleting lymphocytes that react to the pathogen","exposing the immune system to antigen so memory cells form without disease","supplying preformed antibodies that act for a few weeks","boosting the nonspecific innate barriers such as the skin and gastric acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A vaccine presents the antigens of a pathogen (as killed or attenuated forms) so the adaptive system mounts a response and generates memory cells, conferring active immunity and rapid protection on later exposure — without causing the disease itself. — Blood & Immunology, slide 63"},{"q":"Passive immunity, such as antibodies passed in breast milk, is limited because...","choices":["it works only against the recipient's own tissues rather than against pathogens","the recipient makes no memory cells, so protection fades as antibodies degrade","it triggers lifelong immunity through the recipient's own T-cells","it strengthens over time as the antibodies multiply"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In passive immunity the recipient receives ready-made antibodies but mounts no response of their own, so no memory cells form; as the transferred antibodies are gradually degraded, protection wanes within weeks. — Blood & Immunology, slide 63"},{"q":"The enteric nervous system is notable because it...","choices":["can coordinate motility and secretion largely on its own, independent of the CNS","operates solely through circulating hormones in the blood rather than through any neurons","requires constant input from the brain to function at all","controls only the secretion of gastric acid and nothing else along the gut wall"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The enteric nervous system, with its myenteric and submucosal plexuses, contains enough neurons to integrate reflexes and control motility and secretion by itself; the autonomic nervous system modulates it, but the gut can function even without central input. — GI Physiology, slides 24-33"},{"q":"Vagal (parasympathetic) stimulation of the stomach generally...","choices":["increases both gastric motility and acid secretion","decreases motility while raising acid secretion","shuts down all secretion during a meal","acts only on the pancreas, not the stomach"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Parasympathetic (vagal) input, acting largely through acetylcholine, stimulates gastric smooth muscle contraction and enhances secretion, including acid from parietal cells and gastrin release; this underlies the cephalic phase of gastric secretion. — GI Physiology, slides 88-90"},{"q":"The cephalic phase of gastric secretion is triggered by...","choices":["fat and protein reaching the small intestine","stretch of the stomach wall as food enters","the sight, smell, or thought of food acting through the vagus nerve","acidic chyme arriving in the duodenum after the stomach has emptied its load"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The cephalic phase begins before food reaches the stomach: sensory and cognitive cues (sight, smell, taste, thought of food) drive vagal output that stimulates gastric secretion in anticipation of the meal, accounting for roughly a third of the acid response. — GI Physiology, slides 92-95"},{"q":"Pepsin works best in the stomach because it...","choices":["digests dietary carbohydrates rather than protein in the stomach","requires the alkaline environment created by bile","is a protease with an acidic optimal pH provided by gastric HCl","functions only after being neutralized in the duodenum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pepsin is a protein-digesting enzyme with an acidic pH optimum (roughly 1.8–3.5); gastric HCl both activates pepsinogen to pepsin and provides the low pH environment in which pepsin is most active, so it functions well in the stomach and is inactivated in the alkaline duodenum. — GI Physiology, slides 82-85"},{"q":"Between meals, the gallbladder...","choices":["empties continuously to keep the duodenum supplied in the body under most conditions","stores and concentrates bile by absorbing water and electrolytes","manufactures new bile from cholesterol entirely on its own","actively secretes new bile salts into the duodenum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. During fasting the sphincter of Oddi is closed and bile is diverted into the gallbladder, whose mucosa absorbs water and electrolytes to concentrate the bile several-fold; it is then released when a fatty meal triggers CCK. — Ch 71 Liver, slide 17"},{"q":"Pancreatic amylase continues carbohydrate digestion by...","choices":["splitting proteins into individual amino acids","converting absorbed glucose directly into stored glycogen in the gut wall","emulsifying dietary fat into small droplets","breaking starch down into disaccharides within the small intestine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pancreatic amylase, secreted into the duodenum, hydrolyzes starch and glycogen into disaccharides and short oligosaccharides; brush-border enzymes then complete the job by splitting these into absorbable monosaccharides. It is the main luminal enzyme for carbohydrate digestion. — GI Physiology, slides 101, 123-124"},{"q":"Because portal blood passes through the liver before reaching the body, the liver can...","choices":["metabolize and detoxify many absorbed substances before they circulate","return undigested food directly to the stomach","add dissolved oxygen to the blood returning from the gut before it circulates","store the absorbed material permanently as bile"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nutrients and other absorbed substances from the gut drain first through the portal vein into the liver, allowing it to process nutrients, store or release glucose, and detoxify drugs and toxins (first-pass metabolism) before the blood reaches the systemic circulation. — Ch 71 Liver, slides 3, 8"},{"q":"Glucose absorption against its gradient is powered indirectly by...","choices":["bile-salt micelles physically pulling the glucose into the cell","direct hydrolysis of ATP by the glucose carrier itself","the concentration gradient of fructose in the lumen","the sodium gradient maintained by the basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Glucose enters the enterocyte on the SGLT1 cotransporter, riding the inward sodium gradient; that gradient is set up by the basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase, so glucose uptake is secondary active transport ultimately powered by ATP used to pump sodium. — GI Physiology, slide 126"},{"q":"Absorbed dietary fat reaches the systemic circulation by...","choices":["binding tightly to hemoglobin so as to be carried for transport inside the red blood cells","traveling as chylomicrons through lymph and entering blood at the thoracic duct","diffusing straight into the portal vein like glucose","being stored within the liver for a time before any of it is finally released"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Reassembled triglycerides are packaged into chylomicrons, which are too large to enter blood capillaries; they enter the lacteals of the villi, travel through the lymphatic system, and join the bloodstream where the thoracic duct empties into the venous system. — GI Physiology, slides 135-138"},{"q":"Defecation can be voluntarily postponed because...","choices":["the external anal sphincter is skeletal muscle under conscious control","the internal anal sphincter is under full voluntary command","individual mass movements can be consciously stopped anywhere in the colon","the rectum can absorb the feces if defecation is delayed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Although rectal filling relaxes the internal anal sphincter reflexly, the external anal sphincter is striated (skeletal) muscle innervated by the somatic pudendal nerves and under voluntary control, so a person can consciously delay defecation. — GI Physiology, slides 61-68"},{"q":"Failure of the liver to clear ammonia is dangerous because ammonia...","choices":["is harmless and simply excreted unchanged by the lungs","is the main metabolic fuel used by the beating cardiac muscle","is toxic to the nervous system and can impair brain function","is required in large amounts for red cell production"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ammonia produced by amino-acid deamination and gut bacteria is neurotoxic; the liver normally converts it to urea for excretion. When hepatic function fails, ammonia accumulates and can cause hepatic encephalopathy — confusion, altered consciousness, and impaired brain function. — Ch 71 Liver, slide 10; Ch 70, slide 16"},{"q":"During fasting, the liver supports other tissues by...","choices":["storing all fatty acids so they cannot be used","releasing oxygen bound to stored hemoglobin","converting circulating ketones back into stored dietary fat","producing ketone bodies from fatty acids for use as fuel"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In fasting, the liver oxidizes fatty acids and converts the surplus acetyl-CoA into ketone bodies, which it exports as an alternative fuel for tissues such as the brain, sparing glucose during prolonged energy shortage. — Ch 69, slides 15-19; Ch 71, slide 12"},{"q":"The liver uses glucokinase (rather than only hexokinase) to phosphorylate glucose, which allows it to...","choices":["release glucose faster than any other tissue","convert the incoming glucose directly into new protein for the body's use","take up large amounts of glucose after a meal without being saturated","avoid trapping glucose inside its cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Glucokinase has a high capacity and is not readily inhibited by its product, so unlike hexokinase it keeps phosphorylating glucose even at high concentrations; this lets the liver absorb and store large glucose loads after a meal, buffering blood glucose. — Ch 68, slides 4-7"},{"q":"Between meals, the liver keeps blood glucose from falling by...","choices":["converting glucose into glycogen for storage","absorbing glucose taken from resting muscle in order to store it centrally","breaking down stored glycogen and releasing glucose (glycogenolysis)","halting all glucose release to conserve it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In the postabsorptive state the liver depolymerizes its glycogen (glycogenolysis) and, as that store runs low, makes new glucose (gluconeogenesis), releasing glucose into the blood to prevent hypoglycemia. This is its glucose-buffer function. — Ch 71 Liver, slide 9"},{"q":"The pentose phosphate pathway is important mainly because it produces...","choices":["urea, used to dispose of the body's excess nitrogen","NADPH for biosynthesis and ribose for nucleic acids","large amounts of ATP for muscle contraction","lactate to regenerate NAD+ during exercise"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pentose phosphate (hexose monophosphate) shunt diverts glucose-6-phosphate to generate NADPH — the reducing power for fatty-acid and other biosynthesis and for antioxidant defense — and ribose-5-phosphate for nucleotide and nucleic-acid synthesis, rather than making ATP. — Ch 68, slides 28-29"},{"q":"Chylomicrons and VLDL both carry triglyceride, but they differ in that...","choices":["chylomicrons carry dietary fat from the gut, while VLDL carries fat made by the liver","chylomicrons are made in the liver, while VLDL comes from the intestine under these conditions","both particles are made only within adipose tissue purely for local fat storage","chylomicrons carry cholesterol back to the liver, while VLDL carries dietary fat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chylomicrons are assembled in the intestine to transport exogenous (dietary) fat, whereas VLDL is made by the liver to export endogenous triglyceride (much of it synthesized from carbohydrate); both deliver fatty acids to tissues via lipoprotein lipase. — Ch 69, slide 6; GI Physiology, slide 139"},{"q":"Cells take up cholesterol-rich LDL particles by...","choices":["binding them to LDL receptors and internalizing them","allowing LDL to diffuse freely across the membrane","converting LDL into HDL at the cell surface","digesting LDL with pancreatic lipase in the blood"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cells express LDL receptors that bind circulating LDL and bring it in by receptor-mediated endocytosis, delivering cholesterol for membrane and steroid synthesis; too few functional receptors leave LDL in the blood and raise plasma cholesterol. — Ch 69, slide 36"},{"q":"A person in positive nitrogen balance is...","choices":["excreting essentially all dietary nitrogen unchanged in the urine each day","building more body protein than is being broken down, as during growth","losing more nitrogen than is taken in, as during starvation","unable to synthesize any nonessential amino acids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Positive nitrogen balance means nitrogen intake exceeds nitrogen loss, indicating net protein synthesis; it occurs during growth, pregnancy, and recovery/muscle-building, when the body is accumulating protein. — Ch 72, slides 7-8; Ch 70"},{"q":"The urea cycle disposes of nitrogen and is...","choices":["performed in every cell of the body equally","located mainly within the tubular cells of the kidney rather than the liver","independent of any other metabolic pathway","carried out essentially only in the liver and linked to the TCA cycle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Urea synthesis occurs almost exclusively in the liver, where two ammonia molecules and CO2 are combined into urea; the cycle interlinks with the TCA cycle, and excess ammonia is toxic partly because it can deplete TCA intermediates. — Ch 70, slides 16-17"},{"q":"Cortisol influences protein metabolism by...","choices":["blocking the liver from making any new glucose during stress or fasting states","preventing any amino acids at all from entering the blood during stress or fasting states","promoting breakdown of tissue protein to supply amino acids for gluconeogenesis","stimulating net protein synthesis in all tissues"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cortisol (a glucocorticoid) drives catabolism of extrahepatic tissue protein, raising plasma amino acids that the liver uses for gluconeogenesis; this helps maintain blood glucose during stress and fasting. — Ch 70, slide 19; Ch 68, slide 30"},{"q":"In a sedentary person, the largest component of daily energy expenditure is...","choices":["the basal metabolic rate needed to sustain resting functions","shivering to maintain body temperature","the thermic effect of digesting food","the energy cost of occasional vigorous physical exercise sessions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In most sedentary people the basal metabolic rate — the energy to run vital resting processes — accounts for the majority (roughly 50–70%) of daily expenditure, far exceeding the smaller contributions of activity and the thermic effect of food. — Ch 72, slide 2; Ch 73, slide 10"},{"q":"The body's largest energy reserve is stored as...","choices":["triglyceride in adipose tissue","glycogen stored within liver tissue","protein in circulating plasma","free glucose in the blood"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. By far the largest energy store is triglyceride in adipose tissue, which packs about 9 kcal/g with little associated water; a lean adult may carry enough fat for weeks of energy, dwarfing the limited glycogen and glucose reserves. — Ch 72, slide 3; Ch 73, slide 12"},{"q":"Destruction of the lateral hypothalamic nuclei tends to cause...","choices":["excessive eating, since this is the satiety center","reduced eating and weight loss, since this is the feeding center","an inability to regulate body temperature only","essentially no change in appetite or feeding behavior whatsoever"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lateral hypothalamus acts as the feeding (hunger) center; lesions here remove the drive to eat, producing reduced intake and weight loss (inanition). This complements the ventromedial nuclei, which serve as the satiety center. — Ch 72, slide 9"},{"q":"Which is a genuine mechanism of body heat loss?","choices":["Piloerection, the raising of the body hairs to shed heat to the surroundings","Shivering, the production of heat by muscle contraction","Radiation, the emission of infrared heat waves to cooler surroundings","Vasoconstriction, the narrowing of skin vessels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Radiation transfers heat as infrared waves from the warm body to cooler surrounding objects and is a major route of heat loss at rest; the other options listed are heat-conserving or heat-generating responses, not means of losing heat. — Ch 74, slides 6, 13-14"},{"q":"A fever occurs when the hypothalamic set point is...","choices":["raised, so the body activates heat-gaining responses until it reaches the new level","removed entirely by the pyrogen, leaving the body temperature completely uncontrolled now","lowered, so that the body promptly begins to sweat and cool itself back down again","unchanged, with the rise coming only from the environment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pyrogens raise the hypothalamic set point; the body then perceives itself as too cold and triggers heat-gaining responses (vasoconstriction, shivering, chills) until core temperature rises to the new, higher set point — producing fever. — Ch 74, slides 4, 18"},{"q":"Sweating cools the body only when...","choices":["it is reabsorbed back into the sweat glands during this particular process","it remains as liquid droplets on the skin surface","the surrounding air is already fully saturated with water","the sweat actually evaporates, absorbing heat from the skin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cooling comes from the evaporation of sweat, which absorbs heat (the latent heat of vaporization) from the skin; sweat that merely drips off without evaporating removes little heat, which is why humid conditions blunt sweat's cooling effect. — Ch 74, slides 6-9"},{"q":"A respiratory quotient closer to 0.7 indicates that the body is primarily burning...","choices":["no fuel at all, because gas exchange has completely stopped","protein used exclusively, fixing the RQ at 1.0","fat, which yields relatively less CO2 per O2 consumed","carbohydrate, which yields an RQ of about 1.0"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fat oxidation gives a respiratory quotient near 0.7 because oxidizing fatty acids consumes comparatively more oxygen relative to the CO2 produced; this is typical of the fasted state or fat-based metabolism. — Ch 72, slides 5-6"},{"q":"Basal metabolic rate is measured under standardized conditions that include...","choices":["a rested, fasting state at a comfortable, constant temperature","measured during a period of exposure to fairly extreme environmental cold","immediately after vigorous exercise","right after a large protein-rich meal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. BMR is measured after roughly a 12-hour fast, at rest, without excitement, and at a comfortable constant temperature, so that only the minimal maintenance energy is captured without the confounding effects of digestion, activity, or thermal stress. — Ch 73, slide 8"},{"q":"The thermic effect of food refers to...","choices":["the heat lost through sweating during a meal","the extra energy expended to digest, absorb, and process a meal","the energy used only during physical activity","the surplus calories that are stored away as fat after overeating"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The thermic effect of food is the rise in energy expenditure required to digest, absorb, and metabolize nutrients; it is modest for carbohydrate and fat but substantial for protein (which can raise metabolic rate up to about 30%). — Ch 73, slide 10"},{"q":"Sympathetic activation can raise metabolic rate in part by...","choices":["blocking thyroid hormone from acting on tissues","shutting down all cellular metabolism to save energy","stimulating heat production, including thermogenesis in brown fat","lowering the core body temperature in order to reduce metabolic demand"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sympathetic activity increases cellular metabolism and can drive thermogenesis, notably in brown adipose tissue, raising heat production and metabolic rate; this contributes to the metabolic response to cold and stress. — Ch 73, slide 9"},{"q":"During forced (heavy) breathing, accessory muscles such as the sternocleidomastoid and abdominals...","choices":["are used exclusively during quiet resting breaths","assist the diaphragm to move larger volumes of air","act only to slow breathing back to rest","replace the diaphragm, which stops working during exercise"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In quiet breathing the diaphragm suffices, but during forced or heavy breathing accessory muscles are recruited — the sternocleidomastoid and scalenes aid inspiration, and the abdominals and internal intercostals aid expiration — to move much larger volumes. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 12-14"},{"q":"Intrapleural pressure is normally negative (sub-atmospheric), which...","choices":["forces the lungs to collapse against the chest wall","has no effect on whether the lungs stay inflated","actively pushes the air outward during quiet resting inspiration","keeps the lungs expanded against their tendency to recoil inward"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The negative intrapleural pressure results from the lung's inward recoil opposing the chest wall's outward pull; this sub-atmospheric pressure holds the lungs expanded and coupled to the chest wall throughout the breathing cycle. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 14, 17-19"},{"q":"If air enters the pleural space and abolishes the negative pressure, the lung tends to...","choices":["recoil and collapse because nothing opposes its elastic pull","remain unchanged since pleural pressure is irrelevant","stiffen noticeably and become nearly impossible to compress","expand further because recoil is now unopposed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lung's inherent elastic recoil pulls it inward; the negative pleural pressure normally counteracts this. If air enters the pleural space (pneumothorax) and abolishes that negative pressure, the recoil is unopposed and the lung collapses. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 19, 50"},{"q":"Inspiratory reserve volume is the air that...","choices":["can still be exhaled beyond a normal quiet resting expiration","is exhaled during a single quiet resting breath","remains in the lungs after a maximal expiration","can still be inhaled beyond a normal quiet inspiration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inspiratory reserve volume is the additional air that can be forcibly inhaled after a normal tidal inspiration; it is the inspiratory 'reserve' available above the resting breath. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 27-29"},{"q":"Total lung capacity equals...","choices":["tidal volume multiplied by the respiratory rate","the sum of all four lung volumes, including residual volume","the air exchanged in one minute of breathing in the body under most conditions","vital capacity alone, since residual volume is excluded"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Total lung capacity is the maximum air the lungs can hold, equal to the sum of tidal volume, inspiratory reserve, expiratory reserve, and residual volume; because it includes residual volume, it cannot be measured by spirometry alone. — Pulmonary I & II, slide 31"},{"q":"By lowering surface tension, surfactant...","choices":["increases the work of breathing to strengthen the lungs","thickens the fluid layer to slow gas exchange","raises the surface tension evenly across all of the alveoli regardless of size","reduces the effort needed to inflate the lungs and prevents alveolar collapse"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Surfactant lowers alveolar surface tension, which raises compliance (making the lungs easier to inflate) and stabilizes alveoli of different sizes so small ones do not empty into larger ones; without it, the work of breathing rises and alveoli collapse (as in neonatal respiratory distress). — Pulmonary I & II, slides 22-23"},{"q":"Despite a smaller partial-pressure gradient, CO2 crosses the respiratory membrane readily because it is...","choices":["carried across bound to hemoglobin","much less soluble than oxygen, slowing its diffusion","actively pumped across by alveolar cells","far more soluble than oxygen, aiding its diffusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Carbon dioxide is roughly 20 times more soluble than oxygen, so even though its partial-pressure gradient across the membrane is smaller, it diffuses about as readily; solubility is a key factor in Fick's law of diffusion. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 11, 61-62"},{"q":"In a healthy lung at rest, blood in the pulmonary capillary equilibrates with alveolar oxygen...","choices":["never fully, so arterial blood is always desaturated","well before it leaves the capillary, giving a large safety margin","only at the very last instant before leaving the capillary","only during vigorous exercise, and essentially never while at rest"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. At rest, oxygen equilibrates between alveolus and capillary blood in about a third of the transit time, so equilibration is complete long before the blood exits the capillary; this reserve is the safety factor that is eroded in disease or reduced with very fast transit during exercise. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 67-69"},{"q":"Gas exchange across the respiratory membrane is efficient largely because the total alveolar surface is...","choices":["very small but compensated by thickness","large but separated from blood by cartilage","thin but limited to a few large air sacs","very large while the membrane is extremely thin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lungs contain hundreds of millions of alveoli providing an enormous total surface area, and the respiratory membrane is only about 0.2 micrometers thick; large area and short diffusion distance together maximize the rate of gas exchange. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 57-60"},{"q":"Fluid accumulating in the interstitial space of the respiratory membrane would...","choices":["increase the diffusion distance and slow gas exchange","convert the membrane into an active transport surface","have no effect on the rate of gas diffusion","shorten the diffusion distance and speed exchange"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. By thickening the barrier that gases must cross, interstitial or alveolar fluid (as in pulmonary edema) lengthens the diffusion distance; per Fick's law, this reduces the rate of gas exchange and can impair oxygenation. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 60-62"},{"q":"Higher brainstem centers in the pons help shape breathing by...","choices":["generating the basic breathing rhythm entirely on their own","contracting the diaphragm without any nerve involvement","modifying the rhythm set by the medullary centers","sensing arterial oxygen directly like the carotid bodies"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The basic respiratory rhythm arises in the medulla, but pontine centers modulate and smooth it — fine-tuning the transition between inspiration and expiration — so breathing is regular rather than gasping. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 88-90"},{"q":"The peripheral chemoreceptors' oxygen response becomes an important drive to breathe mainly when...","choices":["arterial oxygen falls to notably low levels","blood pressure rises at the carotid sinus","carbon dioxide is the only gas that has changed","arterial oxygen is completely normal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The carotid and aortic bodies respond to low arterial PO2, but this hypoxic drive becomes significant only when PO2 falls well below normal (roughly under 60 mmHg); under normal conditions, CO2 is the dominant stimulus and oxygen contributes little. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 95, 99"},{"q":"At a normal arterial PO2 of about 100 mmHg, hemoglobin is...","choices":["only about half saturated with oxygen","almost fully saturated with oxygen","completely stripped of its oxygen","unable to bind oxygen until PO2 rises higher"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. On the flat upper plateau of the oxygen-hemoglobin curve, a normal arterial PO2 (~100 mmHg) leaves hemoglobin about 97–98% saturated; the plateau ensures saturation stays high even if PO2 dips somewhat, providing a loading safety margin. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 78-80"},{"q":"Carbon monoxide is dangerous partly because, in addition to blocking O2 sites, it...","choices":["makes hemoglobin hold its remaining oxygen more tightly, hindering release to tissues","actually increases the total oxygen-carrying content of the blood delivered to the tissues","causes hemoglobin to release its bound oxygen far too easily before reaching tissue","is readily displaced by the small amount of dissolved oxygen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Carbon monoxide not only occupies binding sites (it binds hemoglobin with far greater affinity than oxygen) but also shifts the dissociation curve so the remaining bound oxygen is released less readily at the tissues, compounding the impairment of oxygen delivery. — Pulmonary I & II, slide 82"},{"q":"During exercise, working muscle extracts more oxygen from each unit of blood because...","choices":["oxygen is actively pumped back out into the blood from the muscle","blood flow to the muscle stops during contraction under these conditions","low tissue PO2 and a rightward curve shift favor greater unloading","hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen rises in active tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Exercising muscle has a very low PO2 and generates heat, CO2, and acid that shift the oxygen-hemoglobin curve rightward; together these steepen the gradient and lower hemoglobin's affinity, so much more oxygen is unloaded per unit of blood than at rest. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 80-81"},{"q":"Carbonic anhydrase speeds CO2 transport because it...","choices":["pumps bicarbonate out of the plasma into the lungs","destroys excess CO2 before it can be transported during this particular process","binds the CO2 molecule directly onto the heme iron of hemoglobin","rapidly converts CO2 and water into carbonic acid inside red cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Carbonic anhydrase in red cells greatly accelerates the hydration of CO2 to carbonic acid, which then dissociates to bicarbonate and H+; this lets the blood carry the large majority of its CO2 as bicarbonate and is central to CO2 transport and acid-base handling. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 83-87"},{"q":"A portion of CO2 is carried bound to hemoglobin as carbaminohemoglobin; this binding occurs at...","choices":["the plasma proteins outside the red cell","the carbonic anhydrase enzyme molecule itself within the cell","the globin portion, not the heme iron where oxygen binds","the same heme iron site used by oxygen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Carbamino CO2 attaches to amino groups on the globin chains of hemoglobin, a different site from the heme iron that binds oxygen; this is why hemoglobin can carry oxygen and some CO2 simultaneously. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 83-86"},{"q":"Voluntary hyperventilation, by removing extra CO2, tends to...","choices":["raise CO2 levels in the blood in the body under most conditions","raise blood pH, producing a respiratory alkalosis","leave blood pH completely unchanged","lower blood pH, producing a respiratory acidosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hyperventilation blows off CO2, lowering carbonic acid and hydrogen ion concentration, so blood pH rises — a respiratory alkalosis. This illustrates how ventilation directly influences acid-base balance through CO2. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 91-92, 100"},{"q":"The tight link between CO2 and pH means that increasing ventilation...","choices":["adds CO2 and thereby raises hydrogen ion concentration","has no relationship to blood acid-base status","removes CO2 and thereby reduces hydrogen ion concentration","boosts oxygen saturation slightly without altering blood pH at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Because CO2 combines with water to form carbonic acid (and thus H+), increasing ventilation removes CO2, lowers carbonic acid, and reduces hydrogen ion concentration, raising pH; this is the respiratory system's rapid lever on acid-base balance. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 91-92"},{"q":"Which correctly describes CO2 movement?","choices":["CO2 diffuses from tissues into blood, then from blood into alveoli","CO2 diffuses from blood into tissues at both sites","CO2 moves from alveoli into blood in the lungs","CO2 is carried in the blood without ever actually leaving it at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Metabolizing tissues have a high PCO2, so CO2 diffuses into the blood; in the lungs, alveolar PCO2 is lower than that of venous blood, so CO2 diffuses from blood into the alveoli to be exhaled. Each step follows the partial-pressure gradient. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 73-74, 86-87"}]};
